 "signs": [
  {"x": 7, "y": 22, "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The Falkland Islands pound is fixed one for one with sterling, and so are the Gibraltar and St Helena pounds. Elsewhere the territories use the US dollar, their own dollars tied to it in Bermuda and the Cayman Islands, the Eastern Caribbean dollar in Anguilla and Montserrat, and the New Zealand dollar on Pitcairn. Small economies borrow a big currency's steadiness.", "kind": "plaque", "dx": 0.475, "plaque": "econ_ot_money", "title": "The Territories' Money"},
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "falklands_islands",
   "title": "The Falkland Islands",
   "text": "Two main islands, East and West Falkland, and hundreds of smaller ones, in the South Atlantic around 500 kilometres off the coast of Patagonia. About 3,600 people live here, and a great many more sheep. The currency is the Falkland pound, at par with sterling."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "falklands_stanley",
   "title": "Stanley",
   "text": "The capital, with about 2,500 people, on a hillside above a long sheltered harbour. It became the capital in 1845, when the governor moved from Port Louis. The islanders call everything outside Stanley 'Camp', from the Spanish 'campo', the countryside. If you have ever heard 'the Camp', that is what is meant."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "falklands_whalebone",
   "title": "The Whalebone Arch",
   "text": "Made from the jawbones of two blue whales, the Whalebone Arch was put up in 1933 to mark a hundred years of British rule in the islands. It stands in front of Christ Church Cathedral. Stanley was once a whaling and sealing port, and the arch is a reminder of what the islands' waters used to hold."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "falklands_cathedral",
   "title": "Christ Church Cathedral",
   "text": "Built of local stone and red brick and completed in 1892, this is the southernmost Anglican cathedral in the world. It is small and plain and loved. Inside are memorials to wrecks and to the sailors lost in them, and in the churchyard a good many Falkland names."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "falklands_1982",
   "title": "1982",
   "text": "In April 1982 Argentine forces invaded the islands, and a British task force sailed 8,000 miles to retake them. After about ten weeks of fighting, Argentine forces surrendered at Stanley on 14 June. 255 British servicemen, 649 Argentine servicemen and three islanders died. The Liberation Monument, unveiled in 1984, lists the names of the British dead."
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "falklands_referendum",
   "title": "The 2013 Referendum",
   "text": "In March 2013 the islanders were asked whether they wished the islands to remain a British Overseas Territory. 1,513 votes were in favour and three were against, a result so lopsided that the count was over before the kettle had boiled."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "falklands_hulks",
   "title": "The Hulks",
   "text": "Stanley Harbour is full of old ships. The iron barque Lady Elizabeth, built in 1879, was damaged off Cape Horn in 1913 and ended her days here as a hulk. The Jhelum, a clipper, was condemned here in 1871. They were used for storage, and many are still visible at low tide, rusting quietly."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "falklands_tin",
   "title": "Tin Roofs",
   "text": "Stanley's houses have corrugated iron roofs in bright blues, reds, greens and yellows, a practical choice in a place where wind blows slates off a roof. Many are clad in timber and painted, and the gardens are full of bulbs and wind-stunted trees. The overall effect is of a coastal village drawn by a child with a lot of paint."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "falklands_peat",
   "title": "Peat",
   "text": "For generations most homes in Stanley burned peat, cut by hand from peat banks in the hills in summer and stacked to dry. Peat fires burn slowly and smell wonderful, a little like a distant bonfire. Some houses still burn it, and the smell on a cold dusk is part of the town."
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "falklands_wool",
   "title": "Wool",
   "text": "Sheep farming has made a living for the islands since the 1860s, and wool is still a key export. Islanders live and work in settlements in Camp, often many miles from the nearest road. Most of the day-to-day traffic is by Land Rover and light aircraft."
  }
 ],
